      "abstract": "The relationship between theory and practice is a key philosophical problem. Since Plato reacted to the sentencing of Socrates to death by separating the contemplative work of theory from the active work of politics, most philosophers\u2014even most political philosophers\u2014have argued that practical applications \u201care not prerequisites of a theory\u201d (Schmidtz 2006, p. 27). Today, these questions have reemerged, as philosophers and political theorists increasingly have to justify the value of theory to both public officials in order to obtain funding and to students worried about their employment prospects. This has led some political philosophers to claim that they should \u201chave socio-political ambitions for their work\u201d (Bright 2021). Going even further, proponents of public philosophy argue that political thinkers \u201chave a responsibility to take matters of public policy seriously\u201d (Wolff 2011, p. 1). The critical theory tradition associated with the Institut f\u00fcr Sozialforschung (Institute for Social Research, hereafter IfS) in Frankfurt is a touchstone for these debates. Established in 1923 following the worker's uprisings that accompanied the end of the Great War in Germany, the Frankfurt Circle sought to understand why the proletarian revolution had failed. Driven by Karl Marx's project of \u201cruthless criticism of the existing order\u201d (in Tucker 1978, p. 788), the first generation sought to fulfill Marx's Eleventh Thesis on Feuerbach by building a bridge between theory and practice. However, on its hundredth anniversary, there is widespread agreement that \u201ccritical theory today is rarely practiced in the sense intended by its founders\u201d (Ibsen 2022, p. 3). This \u201cdomestication\u201d (Thompson 2016) is often attributed to the \u201cneo-idealist\u201d linguistic paradigm J\u00fcrgen Habermas introduced in the 1970s. These critics allege that Habermas \u201cabandoned\u201d (Thornhill 2000, p. 173) the practical aspirations of the original paradigm of the Frankfurt School by creating a \u201cphilosophy which finds peace within itself\u2026 [that] has nothing to do with critical theory\u201d (Rockmore 1989, p. 166). This scholarship presents Habermas as an apostate, who, despite learning from Theodor Adorno, \u201cretreated into a kind of transcendentalism\u201d that sealed critical theory off from practice. Raymond Geuss (1981, p. 94) thus concludes that the Frankfurt School is \u201cbetter off\u201d insofar as it is \u201ccloser to Adorno\u2026than to Habermas,\u201d while other prominent commentators see Habermas's approach as \u201can insidious ruse\u201d (Butler 1995, p. 56). This article pushes back against this tendency to pit \u201cthe \u2018good\u2019 Adorno against the \u2018bad\u2019 Habermas\u201d (Specter 2019, p. 322). In contrast to the dominant interpretation outlined above, I argue that the Frankfurt School's retreat from practice actually dates back to the first generation itself, which lost its faith in the possibility of political change following the rise of National Socialism. I therefore agree with Martin Jay (2023, p. 15) that the Frankfurt School has consistently \u201cstruggled to bridge the gap between radical theory and transformative praxis.\u201d Far from severing the critical theory from practice, I show that Habermas engages in theoretically informed praxis as a public intellectual (see also Verov\u0161ek 2021b, 2022, Forthcoming). Whereas the first generation struggled to conceptualize the link between theory and practice after realizing that the proletariat could no longer serve as a vehicle for the revolution, in his work Habermas builds a theoretically consistent bridge between these two spheres via his theory of the public sphere as well as his activities to improve the quality of public debate within it. My argument proceeds on two levels. First, I contend that Habermas reinvigorates Horkheimer's (1972, p. 199) commitment to \u201ctheory dominated at every turn by a concern for reasonable conditions of life\u201d by shifting its focus away from economic, psychological, and cultural categories in favor of an institutional approach that takes politics seriously. More specifically, I show that Habermas identifies the modern democratic public sphere (\u00d6ffentlichkeit) as the \u201cforce within [the concrete historical situation] able to stimulate change\u201d that Horkheimer (1972, pp. 188\u2013243) was ultimately unable to locate. Second, I demonstrate that Habermas has sought to shape practice as \u201can engaged public intellectual in the very same \u2018political public sphere\u2019 that he theorized as a philosopher\u201d (Pensky 2013, p. 31). By highlighting Habermas's understudied Kleine politische Schriften (\u201cshort political writings\u201d), my basic thesis is that far from having to be drawn out \u201cinto a \u2018real\u2019 world from which it otherwise remains isolated\u201d (Whyman 2019), Habermas himself has already applied his critical theory to real politics. In my view, Habermas's philosophy not only provides a bridge between theory and practice but also serves as a model for how philosophers as engaged public I by the practical ambitions of the first generation of the Frankfurt School and the of this original which ultimately into I the bridge that Habermas between theory and practice in his of critical theory on the public sphere of his of political on the as a public intellectual I also a of his as a public on his in the of the In the I of Habermas for having on the kind of of the first I also on how Habermas's to the of theory and practice within the (Bright in political theory and on his work as a public intellectual for his in the tradition of the Frankfurt In his of Social and the of an for Social Horkheimer out a for the which its had to the of the of the worker's Horkheimer pp. the he on into the of that a concern for the of the philosophy at the of i